= Solution
{parent=a}

An <isometry> of the <Euclidean plane> is a map $T:\mathbb R^2\to\mathbb R^2$ preserving all <Euclidean distances>, $\|T(x)-T(y)\|=\|x-y\|$. Such a map of the whole plane is automatically bijective, and has the form $T(x)=Rx+b$ with $R$ an <orthogonal matrix>.

The classification is **identity, <translations>, <rotations> about a point, <reflections> in a line, and glide <reflections>**. Here a <glide reflection> is a <reflection> followed by a nonzero <translation> parallel to its mirror. The cases preserving <orientation> have $\det R=1$ and are <translations> or <rotations>; the orientation-reversing cases have $\det R=-1$ and are <reflections> or glide <reflections>. The identity is included by allowing a zero <translation> or <rotation> angle.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Put $v=P_2-P_1$ and $w=Q_2-Q_1$. They are nonzero <vectors> with the same <Euclidean norm>. Choose the <rotation matrix> $R$ taking the direction $v/\|v\|$ to $w/\|w\|$; equal lengths then give $Rv=w$. Define
$$
\boxed{t(x)=Q_1+R(x-P_1).}
$$
This is a <translation> by $Q_1-P_1$ followed by a <rotation> about $Q_1$, so it is an <isometry>. Direct substitution gives $t(P_1)=Q_1$ and $t(P_2)=Q_1+w=Q_2$.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

First use the preceding <isometry> $t$ to align the first two points, and put $R_3=t(P_3)$. It remains to align $R_3$ with $Q_3$ while fixing $Q_1,Q_2$. Use <orthonormal coordinates> in which $Q_1=(0,0)$ and $Q_2=(d,0)$, $d>0$, and write $R_3=(r,s)$, $Q_3=(u,v)$. The prescribed <distances> give
$$
r^2+s^2=u^2+v^2,\qquad (r-d)^2+s^2=(u-d)^2+v^2.
$$
Subtracting yields $r=u$, and then $s^2=v^2$. Therefore $Q_3$ is either $R_3$ or its <reflection> across the line through $Q_1,Q_2$. In the first case take $s=t$; in the second compose $t$ with that <reflection>. Both constructions fix the first two images and send the third point to its required image. The argument also covers three collinear points, when the two possible third images coincide. Thus **all three points can be matched by one plane isometry**.

