= Solution
{parent=a}

A <basis of a topology> $\mathcal T$ is a collection $\mathcal B\subseteq\mathcal T$ such that every <open set> is a union of members of $\mathcal B$. Equivalently, for each open $U$ and every $x\in U$, there exists $B\in\mathcal B$ with $x\in B\subseteq U$. The empty union represents the empty set. In particular, the basis covers $X$, and any intersection of two basis elements is itself a union of basis elements.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The following open rectangles form a <basis of a topology> for the <product topology>:
$$
\boxed{\{U\times V:U\in\mathcal T_X,\ V\in\mathcal T_Y\}.}
$$
These <open rectangles in a finite product> cover $X\times Y$, and intersections remain rectangles since $(U_1\times V_1)\cap(U_2\times V_2)=(U_1\cap U_2)\times(V_1\cap V_2)$. One may equivalently take products of elements from a chosen <basis of a topology> in each factor.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Choose $v_0\in V$, using its nonemptiness. For each $u\in U$, the openness of $U\times V$ in the <product topology> supplies open $O_u\subseteq X$, $W_u\subseteq Y$ such that
$$
(u,v_0)\in O_u\times W_u\subseteq U\times V.
$$
Since $v_0\in W_u$, every point of $O_u$ belongs to $U$. Thus $U=\bigcup_{u\in U}O_u$ is open. Interchanging the factors, using a fixed $u_0\in U$, proves $V$ open. Therefore $\boxed{U\in\mathcal T_X,\quad V\in\mathcal T_Y}$. Nonemptiness matters: an empty product is open whatever the other factor is.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**Yes: at least one factor <topology> must be indiscrete.** Suppose instead that both have a nonempty proper <open set>, say $\varnothing\ne U\subsetneq X$ and $\varnothing\ne V\subsetneq Y$. Then
$$
W=(U\times Y)\cup(X\times V)
$$
is open in the <product topology>. Both coordinate projections of $W$ are the full factors, because $U,V$ are nonempty. If $W$ were a rectangle $A\times B$, this would force $A=X$, $B=Y$, hence $W=X\times Y$. But choosing $x\notin U$ and $y\notin V$ gives $(x,y)\notin W$, a contradiction. Thus both factors cannot have nontrivial <open sets>. This is <rectangularity of every product-open set>.

