= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $q:X\to X/\mathcal R$ send each point to its <equivalence class>. The <quotient topology> is
$$
\boxed{U\subseteq X/\mathcal R\text{ is open}\iff q^{-1}(U)\text{ is open in }X.}
$$
It is the finest <topology> on the quotient set making the canonical surjection $q$ continuous. The preimage test, rather than a choice of representatives, defines its <open sets>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use the <equivalence relation> of belonging to the same fibre:
$$
\boxed{x\mathcal R x'\iff f(x)=f(x').}
$$
The map $\bar f:X/\mathcal R\to Y$, $[x]\mapsto f(x)$, is well defined, injective by the definition of the relation, and surjective because $f$ is surjective. It is continuous by the <quotient topology>: for open $V\subseteq Y$, $q^{-1}(\bar f^{-1}(V))=f^{-1}(V)$ is open.

The <quotient space> is <compact> as the continuous image of <compact> $X$, and $Y$ is <Hausdorff>. The <compact-to-Hausdorff continuous bijection theorem> makes $\bar f$ a <homeomorphism>. Indeed, images of closed subsets of a <compact> space are <compact> and hence closed in a <Hausdorff> space, so the inverse is continuous.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Collapse the equator $E=\{(u,v,t)\in S^2:t=0\}$ to one point and identify no other points:
$$
\boxed{p\mathcal R q\iff p=q\ \text{or}\ (p\in E\text{ and }q\in E).}
$$
Each closed hemisphere is a disk; collapsing its boundary gives a sphere. The two boundaries are the same equator, so the resulting spheres meet at the single collapsed point. This is the <sphere with its equator collapsed>.

An explicit verification avoids relying only on the picture. Away from the poles, use $p=(\sqrt{1-t^2}\cos\theta,\sqrt{1-t^2}\sin\theta,t)$ and define
$$
f(p)=\begin{cases}(1-\cos(\pi t),\ \sin(\pi t)\cos\theta,\ \sin(\pi t)\sin\theta),&t\geq0,\\
(-1+\cos(\pi|t|),\ \sin(\pi|t|)\cos\theta,\ \sin(\pi|t|)\sin\theta),&t\leq0.
\end{cases}
$$
At the north and south poles use $(2,0,0)$ and $(-2,0,0)$, respectively. The angular terms tend uniformly to zero at both poles, making these extensions continuous. Both hemisphere formulas agree at the equator, with value $(0,0,0)$. Their images lie on the two specified unit spheres and cover them; away from the common origin the height parameter and angle are recovered uniquely. Thus the fibres are exactly the stated equivalence classes. The preceding compact-to-Hausdorff argument gives $\boxed{S^2/\mathcal R\cong Y}$.

