= Solution
{parent=i}

A $C^1$ <diffeomorphism> $F:U\to V$ is a <bijection> for which both $F$ and $F^{-1}$ are continuously differentiable. If a higher regularity class is specified, both maps must belong to that class. In particular, a <diffeomorphism> is a <homeomorphism>, and the <chain rule> gives $D(F^{-1})(F(x))=(DF(x))^{-1}$.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <inverse function theorem> states: let $F:U\subseteq\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R^n$ be $C^1$, with $U$ open, and suppose $DF(x_0)$ is invertible. Then there exist open neighbourhoods $U_0\subseteq U$ of $x_0$ and $V_0$ of $F(x_0)$ such that $F:U_0\to V_0$ is a $C^1$ <diffeomorphism>. Its inverse derivative satisfies
$$
\boxed{D(F^{-1})(F(x))=(DF(x))^{-1},\qquad x\in U_0.}
$$
The conclusion is local; invertibility of one <Jacobian matrix> does not assert global injectivity.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The map $G$ is $C^1$ on the open ball $B$. Its <Jacobian matrix> is
$$
DG(x)=\begin{pmatrix}(x_3+3)^{-1}&(x_3+3)^{-1}&-(x_1+x_2)(x_3+3)^{-2}\\0&1&0\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix}.
$$
At the origin $G(0)=0$ and $\boxed{\det DG(0)=1/3\ne0}$. The <inverse function theorem> therefore supplies the required <open sets> $V_1\subset B$ and $V_2\subset\mathbb R^3$, both containing zero, with $G:V_1\to V_2$ a <diffeomorphism>. The explicit inverse formula on its image,
$$
G^{-1}(y_1,y_2,y_3)=(y_1(y_3+3)-y_2,\ y_2,\ y_3),
$$
also checks the local conclusion directly.

