= Solution
{parent=a}

For integer-valued <random variables>, the <joint probability mass function> is
$$
\boxed{p_{X,Y}(x,y)=\mathbb P(X=x,Y=y),\qquad x,y\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
The <marginal probability mass functions> sum out the other variable:
$$
\boxed{p_X(x)=\sum_{y\in\mathbb Z}p_{X,Y}(x,y),\qquad p_Y(y)=\sum_{x\in\mathbb Z}p_{X,Y}(x,y).}
$$
All masses are nonnegative, and their sums over the corresponding sample values equal one.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

<Independence> means that for all subsets $A,B\subseteq\mathbb Z$,
$$
\mathbb P(X\in A,Y\in B)=\mathbb P(X\in A)\mathbb P(Y\in B).
$$
For discrete <random variables>, this is equivalent to the pointwise factorization
$$
\boxed{p_{X,Y}(x,y)=p_X(x)p_Y(y)\quad\text{for every }x,y\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
One direction takes singleton sets; the other sums the pointwise identity over $A\times B$.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The five specified masses sum to one, so there are no other possible outcomes. Symmetry gives $\mathbb E X=0$, while $\mathbb E Y=1/4$. The two terms with $Y=1$ have opposite $X$ values and equal masses, so
$$
\mathbb E(XY)=\frac18-\frac18=0,\qquad\boxed{\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=\mathbb E(XY)-\mathbb E X\,\mathbb E Y=0.}
$$
Nevertheless $\mathbb P(X=0,Y=1)=0$, whereas $\mathbb P(X=0)\mathbb P(Y=1)=(1/2)(1/4)=1/8$. Therefore **$X$ and $Y$ are not independent**. They are <uncorrelated random variables>, illustrating that zero <covariance> alone does not imply independence.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Linearity of <expectation> and the vanishing of cross <covariances> for independent variables give
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E\left[\frac1n\sum_{j=1}^nX_j\right]=\mu,\qquad\operatorname{Var}\left(\frac1n\sum_{j=1}^nX_j\right)=\frac1{n^2}\sum_{j=1}^n\sigma^2=\frac{\sigma^2}{n}.}
$$
The <expectation> formula does not require independence; independence is used for the <variance> sum.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Write $\bar X_n=n^{-1}\sum_{j=1}^nX_j$ and apply the stated second-moment <Markov inequality> to $Y=\bar X_n-\mu$. The preceding calculation gives $\mathbb E Y^2=\sigma^2/n$. Thus for every $\varepsilon>0$,
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(|\bar X_n-\mu|>\varepsilon)\leq\frac{\sigma^2}{n\varepsilon^2}\longrightarrow0.}
$$
This is <convergence in probability> to the common mean, proving the finite-variance <weak law of large numbers>. It also covers $\sigma^2=0$, when the bound is identically zero.
